  "final_text": "Both are in place in your local Kibana.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view has one document per HTTP request, with the time in `timestamp`. The useful fields are:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as text.\n- `bytes`: the size of each response.\n- `clientip`: the visitor's IP address.\n- `url` and `request`: the page requested.\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`: source and destination country.\n- `machine.os`: the visitor's operating system.\n- `agent`, `referer`, `extension`, `host` and `tags`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-4a8f9769 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-4a8f9769-web-logs-overview-ff118c730473`): it has 8 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code\n- A pie chart of the share of each response code\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- A bar chart of requests by source country\n- A pie chart of requests by operating system\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4a8f9769-web-logs-overview-ff118c730473\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-4a8f9769 5xx spike\"** (id `615168fd-959f-4a40-9d7e-2fb9c16f5c97`): it runs every minute and searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` on `timestamp`. It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" and fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes. It's enabled and showed \"pending\" when created, which means it hadn't run yet.\n\nI couldn't test the 5xx filter against the data, because Kibana's console proxy is turned off on this instance. The filter should work, since the sample data stores codes as strings like \"503\", but the rule hasn't been confirmed to fire.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it records an alert in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone. You can add a connector, such as Slack or email, if you want to be notified.",
